body:
Problem

 The forceEnpassantSquare option in fen() doesn't work as documented when there's no adjacent enemy pawn that can capture en passant.

 Current behavior:
 const chess = new Chess();
 chess.move('e4');
 console.log(chess.fen({ forceEnpassantSquare: true }));
 // Expected: "rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/4P3/8/PPPP1PPP/RNBQKBNR b KQkq e3 0 1"
 // Actual:   "rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/4P3/8/PPPP1PPP/RNBQKBNR b KQkq - 0 1"

 Root Cause

 In _makeMove() (around line 1838 in src/chess.ts), the code only sets this._epSquare if there's an adjacent enemy pawn:

 if (move.flags & BITS.BIG_PAWN) {
   let epSquare = us === BLACK ? move.to - 16 : move.to + 16

   // Only sets _epSquare if enemy pawn can capture
   if (
     (!((move.to - 1) & 0x88) && this._board[move.to - 1]?.type === PAWN && ...) ||
     (!((move.to + 1) & 0x88) && this._board[move.to + 1]?.type === PAWN && ...)
   ) {
     this._epSquare = epSquare
     this._hash ^= this._epKey()
   } else {
     this._epSquare = EMPTY  // <-- Problem: sets to EMPTY
   }
 }

 Then in fen() (line 871), it checks if (this._epSquare !== EMPTY) before using forceEnpassantSquare. Since _epSquare is EMPTY, the option has no effect.

 Use Case

 The https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Forsyth%E2%80%93Edwards_Notation has two interpretations:
 1. Strict: Only include en passant square if a legal capture exists
 2. Permissive: Always include en passant square after two-square pawn moves

 Many chess libraries (e.g., Gera.Chess, python-chess with certain options) and PGN standards use the permissive interpretation. The forceEnpassantSquare option should enable this.

 Proposed Fix

 Always set _epSquare for big pawn moves, then let fen() decide whether to include it based on forceEnpassantSquare:

 if (move.flags & BITS.BIG_PAWN) {
   const epSquare = us === BLACK ? move.to - 16 : move.to + 16
   this._epSquare = epSquare
   this._hash ^= this._epKey()
 }
